% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 7
% composition-elements: minor triads (Dm, Gm and Am, i-iv-v in D natural minor); marcato on the leader's entries (bars 1, 3) and on every hit of the stamp break (bars 5-6); a written melodic turn (four sixteenths around one note) in bars 1, 3 and 7 that students vary; a stamp break of marcato quarters and quarter rests in bars 5-6 that leaves beats for an original weight-transfer and shoulder-isolation movement; leader-and-line call and answer
% composition-listen: Omar Souleyman — "Warni Warni" (from the album Wenu Wenu, produced by Kieran Hebden (Four Tet), Ribbon Music, 2013)
% composition-listen-url: https://music.apple.com/us/song/warni-warni/677848558
% composition-bridge: Hear relentless electronic pulse, ornamented vocal/keyboard lines and music made for communal line dancing.
% composition-context: Omar Souleyman's music relates Syrian wedding performance, dabke and electronic production for global stages. This original minor dance study is not dabke music and supplies no traditional steps.
% composition-distance: Original melody, harmony and movement plan; no synth line, vocal phrase or beat from the recording is copied, and no traditional dabke step is taught.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"3","role":"melody leader","prompt":"Change the turn","choices":["turn from above, as written","turn from below","no turn: one long note"],"replyBars":1}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"5-6","role":"line leader","prompt":"Signal in the gaps","choices":["one marcato clap on beat 2","a rising pickup into beat 3","keep the gaps silent"],"replyBars":2}
% dossier-function: original minor line-dance study for Grade 7: a leader plays the turn figure, the line answers, and in the stamp break the class transfers weight on the marcato hits and isolates the shoulders in the rests; it pairs with listening to Omar Souleyman, it does not teach dabke
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4, D natural minor (D E F G A Bb C), written range D4-D5; chords Dm | Dm | Gm | Am | Dm | Gm | Gm-Am | Dm; quarter, eighth and sixteenth notes, a half note and quarter rests; bars 1-2 leader's phrase with the turn Bb-A-G-A, closing on D4; bars 3-4 the line's answer on Gm with the turn C-Bb-A-Bb, closing on A4 (open); bars 5-6 the stamp break, marcato D5, A4, Bb4, G4 on beats 1 and 3 with rests on 2 and 4 and an A4 pickup; bars 7-8 the turn returns and the phrase cadences F-E-D4; largest leap a perfect fifth (D4-A4, bar 1)
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 126 is the dance target; start at 96 and keep the sixteenth turns light. D minor is the rehearsal key; the written range D4-D5 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5. Teach in steps: clap the rhythm of bar 1, sing the turn on "da", play bars 1-4 as leader and line, then add the stamp break standing still before adding the movement. Movement is optional and adaptable: seated players can tap the hits and roll the shoulders in the rests
% dossier-accompaniment: The written Dm, Gm and Am triads drive the page's pop style (piano, drum kit, bass), a generic pop groove chosen so the class hears a steady electronic-age pulse without pretending to be Syrian wedding music or Souleyman's synth and drum-machine sound. Keep the band light in bars 5-6 so the stamps are heard. No beat, keyboard line or sample from the recording is reproduced
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental: recorder, keyboard, bells or voices on "da". No words are supplied, in Arabic or English; no words from the recording are used. Any words a class adds are a class adaptation, not a translation
% dossier-honesty: Original AI-assisted course composition generated for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, rhythm and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on the reference's melody or on any dabke tune; the movement suggestions are generic classroom movement, not dabke steps. The listening reference is a bridge only. Drafted as "Dabke Drive"; retitled because the piece is explicitly not dabke
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, chords, movement plan, improvisation cues and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Warni Warni" and the Wenu Wenu recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included
% dossier-respect: Locate Syria and the Levant, and Souleyman's region of north-east Syria, on a map; explain that dabke is a social line dance with many regional forms in Syria, Lebanon, Palestine, Jordan and Iraq, danced at weddings and community celebrations, and that a concert video shows one artist's international stage career, not the whole practice. Teach dabke steps only with a culture bearer; never present this classroom study, or its stamp break, as dabke
% dossier-review: Syrian or Levantine dabke educator, Grade 7 music/dance teacher, second score reader and student listening panel; all reviews still required
