N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: bar 1 (Dm) marcato D4 quarter, A4 quarter, sixteenth turn Bb-A-G-A, F4 quarter; bar 2 (Dm) G-F-E-F eighths, D4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 3 (Gm) marcato G4, Bb4, turn C5-Bb-A-Bb, G4; bar 4 (Am) C5-A-G-E eighths, A4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 5 (Dm) marcato D5 and A4 on beats 1 and 3, rests on 2 and 4; bar 6 (Gm) marcato Bb4 and G4 on 1 and 3, rest on 2, an eighth rest and an A4 pickup on 4; bar 7 (Gm then Am) G4 quarter, turn Bb-A-G-A, E4 and A4 quarters; bar 8 (Dm) F-E eighths, D4 half, quarter rest. Every bar totals eight eighths (the turns are four sixteenths); the only pitches are D E F G A Bb C; written range D4-D5; largest leap a perfect fifth. The batch draft (titled "Dabke Drive") repeated one quarter-quarter-dotted rhythm in every bar and claimed triads with no chords; this version adds the chords, the turns and the stamp break. Originality check: the opening is not the opening of "Misirlou", "Hava Nagila", "Lamma Bada" or any Souleyman hook, and turn figures around one note are common property of many minor-mode dance musics.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether the sixteenth turns are clean at quarter note = 126 with Grade 7 players, whether the stamp break's rests are long enough for a weight transfer, and whether the pop style reads as a neutral pulse have not been tested. A Levantine dabke educator must judge whether the framing and the new title avoid presenting the study as dabke; a classroom trial, a second score reader and a student listening panel remain required before publication.
